window.__saqPaperQuestions("2026-1-metro-north-sunshine-coast",[{"number":1,"title":"Thoracotomy","stemText":"You work at a Tertiary Hospital. A 54-year-old man has been involved in an altercation at the pub. He has a single left parasternal chest stab wound, with no other injuries. On arrival to ED his observations are:\nGCS 11 – E3V3M5\nHR 150bpm reg\nBP 70/40\nRR 26\nSa02 90% 15L NRB","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State your FIVE (5) immediate management priorities (5 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Shortly after arrival, the patient arrests.\n\nList THREE (3) accepted indications for a resuscitative thoracotomy in the pre-hospital or ED setting. (3 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Complete the following table by listing ONE (1) Pro and Con regarding each surgical"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>MODEL ANSWER<br>Activate Trauma respond /assemble trauma team<br>Damage control resuscitation: Large bore access, Activate MTP, commence blood product resuscitation, permissive hypotension aiming SBP 90 – Up to 2 marks for this point if separate valid points listed<br>Chest decompression: Bilateral finger thoracostomies +/- ICC<br>Improve oxygenation – Prepare for physiologically difficult RSI, RSI (with some qualification that pt needs attempted resuscitation before RSI) <br>Initial investigation: prioritise eFAST to establish if cardiac tamponade (1/2 mark for CXR) <br>Prepare for resuscitative thoracotomy in event of deterioration or unresponsive shock<br>Early notification local Surgical/ trauma service <br><br>Do not accept <br>-\tAnalgesia eg 50mcg fentanyl <br>-\tNeedle decompression <br>-\tExcessive crystalloid resuscitation without qualifying – “Cautious low volume 10ml/kg NaCl 0.9% whilst waiting for blood products availability” <br>-\tCT <br>-\tAlternate methods of supplemental oxygen (eg NIV) <br></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>MODEL ANSWER<br>1.\tPenetrating thoracic injury <br>2.\tRecent Signs of life (+/- within 15 minutes) - EAST<br>3.\tPulseless <br>4.\tEvidence of cardiac tamponade on FAST <br><br>Will accept <br>-\tBlunt injury AND SOL &lt; 10 min (WTA guideline but must time to SOL must be explicitly clarified) <br>-\tProfound unresponsive shock (WTA guideline) AND penetrating thoracic injury <br></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Approach</strong></th><th><strong>Pro</strong></th><th><strong>Con</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>Clamshell</strong></td><td>Better visibility and exposure<br>Can access R side of chest for R thoracic haemorrhage control (not the primary indication of a thoracotomy)<br>No statically significant difference in total time between procedures</td><td>Significantly worse morbidity if survive.<br>No mortality benefit over Left anterior approach in experienced hands</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Left Lateral</strong></td><td>Less morbidity in survivors with equivalent mortality<br>Definitive surgical repair simpler<br>Less confronting for team</td><td>Poorer visibility in less experienced hands<br>Unable to access R side of chest </td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"Paeds Ortho","stemText":"A child is brought to your ED having fallen from some monkey bars. He has a swollen right elbow and appears distressed. You obtain an urgent x-ray shown below.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the table with the diagnosis of the injury and provide TWO (2) radiological findings that support your diagnosis (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You perform a distal neurological exam to assess for injury.\n\nComplete the table below with the TWO (2) most common injured"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State the estimated age of this child and provide a justification from the radiological findings. (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Your assessment does not reveal any neurovascular issue and the injury is closed.\n\nState THREE (3) treatments you would provide for this child (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:inlafaa38467816f19ee"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Diagnosis</strong></th><th><strong>Diagnosis</strong></th><th>Supracondylar fracture, Gartland type 2</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Radiological Finding</strong></td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Fracture line through ant distal humerus with posterior displacement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Radiological Finding</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Posterior cortex intact</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Nerve</strong></th><th><strong>Nerve</strong></th><th><strong>Motor Deficit</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Anterior interosseous nerve</td><td>Loss of thumb IP flexion (loss of ‘OK’ sign)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Radial nerve</td><td>Loss of thumb extension (‘thumb up’ sign)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Estimated age</strong></th><th>Approx 3 years</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Justification</strong></td><td>Based on presence of capitellum ossification centre but lack of radial head one</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Note:  this may be a problematic question/answer.  Happy with anything that suggests a younger child based on the absence of ossification centres</em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Analgaesia for child – eg IN Fentanyl 1.5microg/kg<br>Reduction of fracture – arm flexed to 90’, with inhaled nitrous sedation<br>Long-arm backslab</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"Toxic Alcohol","stemText":"A 60-year-old man is brought in by ambulance to your tertiary Emergency Department after being found unconscious at home. He has a known history of alcohol abuse and was found with a number of unlabelled containers of unknown contents surrounding him at home.\n\nOn arrival his GCS is 5/15, pupils equal 3 mm. Temp 36.7 C, RR 46 per minute, BP 144/60, HR 77 bpm.\n\nThere are no signs of trauma and a CT brain is normal.\n\nA VBG is available:\nTest Result Ref Range\npH 6.77 7.32 - 7.43\npCO2 10 38 - 54 mmHg\nHCO3 2 30 - 32 mmol/L\nBE - 30 0+/-3\nCl 97 95 - 110 mmol/L\nNa 140 135 -145 mmol/L\nK 7.4 3.5 - 5.2 mmol/L\nIon Ca 0.9 1.12-1.32 mmol/L\nLac 25 0.5 -2.2 mmol/L\nGlucose 6 3.5-7.8 mmol/L","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Analyse the acid-base disturbance, including any calculations. (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the most likely toxicological diagnosis and provide three features that support your answer. (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Complete the following table with THREE (3) further investigations that would confirm your diagnosis or assess the severity of the ingestion. For each provide a justification (3 Marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List TWO (2) additional specific treatments (excluding airway management and fluids) for your working diagnosis and briefly justify their role. (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>1 Mark <br>Life threatening HAGMA with AG 41 (140 - 97  -2)<br>1 Mark <br>Delta ratio = change AG/change bicarb = 29/22 = 1.3 = pure HAGMA.<br>1 Mark<br>Resp compensation = 1.5 x HCO2 + 8 = 11 = appropriate respiratory compensation</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Toxicological diagnosis </strong>(1 Mark)<br>Ethylene glycol<br><br><strong>Supporting features</strong> (any three, 1 mark each):<br>Severe HAGMA, <br>Elevated lactate with possible lactate gap<br>Low ionised calcium, <br>Unlabelled containers suggesting ingestion. <br>Normal glucose rules out DKA</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Investigation</strong></th><th><strong>Investigation</strong></th><th><strong>Justification</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1 mark for</strong></td><td><strong>1 mark for</strong></td><td><strong>1 mark for</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Measured serum osmolality</td><td>calculate osmolar gap</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1 mark for each of the following (max 2)</strong></td><td><strong>1 mark for each of the following (max 2)</strong></td><td><strong>1 mark for each of the following (max 2)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Ethylene glycol level</td><td>Confirm ingestion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Creatinine / eGFR</td><td>renal injury severity</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Urinalysis</td><td>calcium oxalate crystals</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>ECG </td><td>hyperkalaemia effects</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Urea</td><td>To calculate serum osmolality </td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Treatment</strong></th><th><strong>Treatment</strong></th><th><strong>Role in treatment</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Fomepizole</td><td>Inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Haemodialysis</td><td>removes toxin and corrects acidosis</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Ethanol administration</td><td>competes with metabolism of ethylene glycol.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Sodium bicarbonate</td><td>correct severe acidaemia</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Calcium</td><td> treat hyperkalaemia / hypocalcaemia</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thiamine/pyridoxine</td><td>shift metabolism away from oxalate</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"OHSS","stemText":"A 30-year-old female presents to your ED with abdominal pain, abdominal swelling, and shortness of breath. She reveals she is currently undertaking fertility treatment and has just undergone egg retrieval 7 days ago.\n\nYou suspect the patient may have ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) risk factors associated with the development of OHSS? (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the TWO (2) main physiological characteristics that drive the development of OHSS? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Complete the following table with (2) clinical features for each of the severity categories of OHSS. (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List TWO (2) life threatening complications of critical OHSS. (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Polycystic ovarian syndrome <br>Previous OHSS <br>Younger age &lt; 35 <br>Low body weight&lt; 60kg <br>Large number of oocyte collected (&gt;17) <br>Rapid rising / high oestrogen levels (&gt; 10,000 pmol/L) <br>High treatment dose of FSH <br>Elevated baseline measurements of anti-mullerian hormone (AMH) <br>Increased ovarian volume and high antral follicle count on baseline scan <br>Hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Ovarian enlargement <br>Increased vascular permeability </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Category</strong></th><th><strong>Features</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>Mild </strong></td><td>Abdominal bloating <br>Mild abdominal pain <br>Ovarian size &lt; 8cm <br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Moderate </strong></td><td>Moderate abdominal pain <br>Nausea +/- vomiting <br>Ultrasound evident ascites <br>Ovarian size 8 – 12cm <br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Severe </strong></td><td>Clinical ascites +/- pleural or pericardial effusion <br>Oligouria (&lt; 30ml/hr) <br>Haematocrit &gt; 0.45 <br>Hyponatraemia (Serum Na &gt; 135 mmol/L) <br>Hypo-osmolality (Osmolality &lt; 282 mOsm/kg) <br>Hyperkalaemia (Serum K &gt; 5 mmol/L) <br>Hypoproteinaemia (Serum albumin &lt; 35g/L) <br>Ovarian size &gt; 12cm <br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Critical </strong></td><td>Tense ascites +/- large pleural or pericardial effusion <br>Haematocrit &gt; 0.55 <br>White cell count &gt; 25,000/ml <br>Thromboembolism <br>Acute respiratory distress syndrome <br></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\">0.5 marks for each feature</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Venous thromboembolism: DVT and/or PE <br>Arterial thrombosis: IJV thrombosis and stroke <br>Renal failure <br>Respiratory failure / ARDS <br>Cerebral oedema <br>Ovarian torsion <br>Ileus <br>Pericardial effusion leading to haemodynamic instability </th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"HUS","stemText":"A 4-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with 2 days of lethargy and reduced urine output. He had a 5-day history of abdominal pain and bloody diarrhoea one week ago, which has now resolved. There is no significant past medical history.\n\nHis Blood tests are shown below:\nTest Result Ref. Range\nHb 78 g/L 110 - 140\nWCC 14.5 x 10⁹/L 5 - 15\nPlatelets 42 ×10⁹/L 150-400\nReticulocytes 6% 0.5 - 2.5%\nBlood film Schistocytes present\nLDH 1650 U/L 100 - 250\nHaptoglobin Undetectable 0.3 - 2.0 g/L\nTotal bilirubin 48 µmol/L (predominantly indirect) <20\nCreatinine 210 µmol/L 20 - 70\nUrea 18 mmol/L 2.5 - 6.5\nCoags INR 1.1, APTT Normal\nDirect antiglobulin test Negative","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Analyse the patients blood tests (3 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the most likely diagnosis and provide TWO (2) justifications to support this diagnosis (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Complete the following table with THREE (3) important differential diagnoses that could present with a similar haematological picture. For each outline one distinguishing feature (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA)<br>Anaemia (Hb 78 g/L)<br>Schistocytes on blood film<br>↑ LDH<br>↓ Haptoglobin<br>Indirect hyperbilirubinaemia<br>Reticulocytosis<br>Thrombocytopenia (Platelets 42 ×10⁹/L)<br>Acute kidney injury<br>Elevated creatinine and urea<br><em>Full marks require recognition of MAHA + thrombocytopenia + AKI and understanding this represents a thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA).</em></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Diagnosis (1 mark)<br>Shiga-toxin associated Haemolytic Uraemic Syndrome <br><br>Justification (2 marks):<br>Recent bloody diarrhoea<br>Classical triad (MAHA, thrombocytopenia, AKI)<br>Age group typical<br>Normal coagulation profile</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th></th><th><strong>Important differential</strong></th><th><strong>Distinguishing feature</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)</td><td>Prominent neurological signs<br>Less marked renal failure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)</td><td>Abnormal coagulation studies (↑ INR/APTT, ↓ fibrinogen)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Sepsis with MAHA</td><td>Fever / other signs of sepsis</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"Geriatrics","stemText":"An 82 year old woman is brought into the Emergency Department from a Residential Aged Care Facility following 2 unwitnessed falls and confusion. Staff report she has had reduced oral intake, nausea and increasing lethargy over the past week.\n\nPMH: AF, HFpEF, Alzheimer’s Dementia, T2DM, Osteoporosis, HTN\n\nMedications:\n• Digoxin 125mcg daily\n• Metoprolol 25mg BD\n• Empagliflozin 25mg daily\n• Amitriptyline 25mg nocte\n• Ramipril 10mg daily\n• Frusemide 40mg daily\n• Risperidone 0.5mg daily, PRN for agitation","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the following table with FOUR (4) medications that may have contributed to her fall and briefly explain how each medication increases fall risk (8 marks)."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List FOUR (4) pharmacokinetic factors in frail, elderly patients that contribute to increased risk of medication toxicity (4 marks)."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>*Must have drug and one correct mechanism to achieve full 2 marks for each drug, do not given a mark for just mentioning the drug from the list above*</em></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th></th><th><strong>Drug</strong></th><th><strong>Mechanism of increasing falls risk</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Digoxin</td><td>AV nodal blockade leading to hypotension and fall, increased vagal tone, arrhythmias (VT, VF), side-effects of toxicity (confusion, weakness, vision issues)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Empagliflozin</td><td>Causes euglycaemic ketoacidosis in acute illness, nocturia / polyuria causing increased trips to bathroom and falls, hypotension, orthostatic hypotension, volume depletion through osmotic diuresis </td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Amitriptyline</td><td>Anticholinergic toxicity – contributes to falls, confusion, urinary retention, hypotension, blurred vision, slow reaction times<br>Sodium channel blockade – arrhythmias <br>Alpha blocking – orthostatic hypotension</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Ramipril</td><td>Postural hypotension, accumulate in renal failure<br></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Metoprolol</td><td>AV nodal blockade – bradycardia, hypotension, orthostatic hypotension, vasovagal syndrome</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Risperidone</td><td>Alpha blockade – hypotension<br>Extrapyramidal side-effects (stiffness, rigidity, shuffling gait) leading to gait instability </td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Frusemide</td><td>Volume depletion, electrolyte disturbance, orthostatic hypotension</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Reduced renal clearance/excretion  – age-related decline in GFR 🡪 accumulation of renally-excreted drugs such as digoxin, lithium, metformin<br>Absorption – polypharmacy may impact absorption (incr or decr) <br>Lower total body water 🡪 smaller volume of distribution of water-soluble drugs 🡪 higher plasma concentration of hydrophilic drugs (e.g. digoxin) <br>Reduced albumin 🡪 protein binding differences. <br>Reduced hepatic clearance 🡪 increased bioavailability and half-life of benzos, opioids, beta-blockers <br>Altered drug receptor sensitivity (↑ CNS sensitivity to sedatives)<br>Impaired homeostatic mechanisms (reduced baroreceptor response)</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"LFTs","stemText":"A 68-year-old man presents to your tertiary Emergency Department with 24 hours of fever, rigors, RUQ pain and vomiting. He has no significant PMHx or medication allergies.\n\nOn examination, he is visibly jaundiced. His vitals are:\nTemp: 38.9C\nRR: 22\nSats: 97% RA\nHR: 101 bpm\nBP: 92 / 58 mmHg\nGCS: 14 [E4 V4 M6]\n\nBlood tests are shown below:\nTest Result Reference Range\nALP 620 U/L 30 – 110\nGGT 810 U/L <38\nALT 210 U/L <34\nAST 185 U/L <31\nTotal Bilirubin 145 umol/L <20\nAlbumin 30 g/L 35 – 50\nCreatinine 210 umol/L 45 – 90\nINR 1.6 0.9 – 1.2","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the pattern of liver function abnormality and TWO (2) features that support this interpretation. (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the MOST LIKELY diagnosis based on the clinical presentation and investigations (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List THREE (3) features in this case that indicate organ dysfunction. (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State TWO (2) alternative diagnoses that could produce a similar liver function pattern. (2 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"State FOUR (4) immediate management priorities in the Emergency Department for this patient? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Pattern of LFT abnormality</strong> (1 Mark)<br>Cholestatic pattern of liver injury (1 mark)<br><br><strong>TWO (2) supportive features</strong> (1 Mark)<br>Marked Elevation ALP, GGT<br>Significant hyperbilirubinaemia with only moderate transaminase elevation </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Acute ascending cholangitis </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>AKI – raised creatinine <br>Coagulopathy - deranged INR indicating hepatic dysfunction <br>Altered mental state<br>Hypotension – shock physiology (persistent despite fluids/ requiring vasopressors)</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Malignant biliary obstruction <br>Drug-induced cholestatic liver injury <br>Benign biliary stricture <br>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Early IV broad-spectrum antibiotics (1 mark) plus 1 mark for correct Abx / Drug doses<br>Amoxycillin / Ampicillin 2 g PLUS Gentamicin / Tobramycin 4-7 mg/kg IV<br>Sepsis Resuscitation – any of the following (1 mark)<br>Aggressive haemodynamic resuscitation (crystalloid / vasopressors) aiming for MAP &gt; 65mmHg<br>Early source control (1 mark)<br>Urgent biliary drainage (ERCP) <br>Surgical / Gastro referral for ERCP<br>Supportive care (1 mark only)<br>Blood Cultures <br>Analgesia with route / dose</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"BURN","stemText":"An 18 year old otherwise fit and healthy 70 Kg male is transported to your regional hospital following a blast injury whilst throwing gasoline onto a bonfire whilst intoxicated.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List FOUR (4) specific life-threatening injuries you would assess for given this mechanism of injury (2 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"During your assessment the patient becomes acutely stridulous and agitated. You elect to emergently intubate the patient\n\nState THREE (3) Anticipated difficulties with managing this patient’s airway. For each provide a modification to your standard approach to address the difficulty (6 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Once he has been stabilised you note he has partial thickness burns to his face, trunk, both anterior upper limb and both anterior lower limbs.\n\nCalculate his TBSI burn and provide his fluid regime over the first 8 hours. Provide any calculations used (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>A- Inhalation Burn\nB- Pulmonary contusion, Haemothorax/Pneumothorax\nC – Solid organ injury, Cardiac contusion <br>D- ICH, Diffuse axonal injury\nE- Spinal fracture, Full thickness burns</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th></th><th><strong>Anticipated difficulty</strong></th><th><strong>Modification</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Swollen airway</td><td>Smaller tube, 2 team set up for front of neck access</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Difficult view from deposited soot / secretions</td><td>Large bore suction, consider SALAD technique</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Difficult view from anatomic distortion</td><td>Most experienced / video laryngoscopy, FONA team</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Difficult BVM from burn and agitation</td><td>2 person technique, DSI to assist with agitation</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Restricted ROM if in collar</td><td>Manual in line stabilisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th></th><th><strong>Calculation</strong></th><th><strong>Result</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>TBSI (%)</strong></td><td>Face 9%, UL 4.5% each, LL 9% each, Torso 18%</td><td>54%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fluid regime over first 8 hours</strong></td><td>Modified Parklands formula of 4ml x weight x %TBSI \n4ml x 70kg x 54% = 15120ml or ~15L<br>50% in first 8 hours</td><td>7.5L Ringers lactate, Hartmanns or Plasmalyte</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"Neonatal vomiting","stemText":"A 15 day old boy is brought into the ED with vomiting and mild lethargy. He was born at term, has been breast fed since and has been well.\n\nHis vital signs on arrival are as below:\nTemp 36.8\nSats 98% on RA\nRR 30\nHR 140\nBP 90/60","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List THREE (3) red flags in the history and 3 red flags in the physical examination that you would look for in order to identify life threatening causes of vomiting in a neonate (6 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Bedside testing showed a BSL of 3.1mmol/L and Ketones of 6. You are concerned about a metabolic cause (inborn error of metabolism) for his symptoms.\n\nComplete the following table with TWO (2) other"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State TWO (2) steps in your treatment of a possible inborn error of metabolism in a neonate (2 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>History (3 marks):<br>Bilious vomiting→ suggests obstruction (e.g. malrotation/volvulus)<br>Projectile vomiting -&gt; Pyloric stenosis (a little young for this px)<br>Lethargy / poor feeding / not waking for feeds → possible sepsis or metabolic disorder<br>Fever or temperature instability → neonatal sepsis<br>Poor weight gain / weight loss<br>Family Hx of neonatal deaths or metabolic disorders<br>Complicated pregnancy (e.g. HELLP, maternal diabetes)<br>Examination (3 marks):<br>Abdominal distension→ obstruction/NEC<br>Signs of shock (poor perfusion, delayed cap refill, hypotension)<br>Altered tone or reduced responsiveness → metabolic/sepsis<br>Jaundice<br>Absent bowel sounds / tinkling bowel sounds<br>Hepatomegaly / splenomegaly<br>Increased work /rate of breathing</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Differential</strong></th><th><strong>Differential</strong></th><th><strong>Associated sign / symptom</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Sepsis</td><td>Fever / Tachycardia / localising sign</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Malrotation +/- volvulus</td><td>Bilious vomiting</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Other acceptable differentials:</em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Congenital adrenal hyperplasia → shock, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pyloric stenosis → projectile non-bilious vomiting</div><div class=\"answer-line\">NEC → bloody stools</div><div class=\"answer-line\">UTI → fever, irritability</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>1 Mark for <br>IV Glucose 10% 2-5mls /kg and then ongoing infusion to maintain BSL<br>1 Mark for any 1 of<br>IV fluids 0.9 % normal saline for hydration, maintainence rate<br>Cease PO intake<br>Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics (until sepsis excluded) - cefotamine or ceftriaxone</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"Frequent Presenter","stemText":"A 34-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with lower abdominal and pelvic pain. She reports pelvic pain for the past 3 years, with intermittent exacerbations. She has attended your ED on 14 occasions in the past 12 months with similar presentations. Previous investigations including multiple pelvic ultrasounds, CT abdomen/pelvis, STI screening and diagnostic laparoscopy have not identified a serious cause.\n\nShe has a history of endometriosis, depression, and a gastric sleeve. Current medications include oxycodone 10 mg QID, amitriptyline 25 mg nocte, and sertraline 100 mg daily.\n\nOn this presentation she describes worsening pain over 48 hours.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) features in the history or examination that would indicate a time-critical cause of her pain. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) factors that are associated with frequent presenters to the ED"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List four (4) management principles relevant to frequent presenters in the Emergency Department. (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>This question is really asking what the candidate’s emergent differential is (eg. Ectopic, torsion, PID/abscess, appendix, pyelo/infected stone, bowel obstruction etc)<br>Hypotension, tachycardia out of proportion, tachypnoea, hypoxia, altered level of consciousness (<strong>only one mark for a vital sign</strong>)<br>Peritonism (rebound, guarding, rigidity)<br>Positive pregnancy test<br>Fever<br>Cervical motion tenderness<br>PV discharge/bleeding (not menses)<br>Sudden onset severe unilateral pain<br>Intractable vomiting with abdominal distension<br>Other options that are sensible<br><br>References:<br>Tintinalli JE et al. Tintinalli’s Emergency Medicine: A Comprehensive Study Guide, 9th ed.<br>RANZCOG. Management of Acute Pelvic Pain Guidelines.<br> </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Factors associated with frequent presenters:<br>Male sex<br>Older age<br>Attendance outside daytime hours<br>Substance abuse<br>Psychosocial problems<br>Intellectual diability<br>Somatic delusions<br>Chronic medical problems<br>Used multiple health services regularly<br> <br>References:<br>·         Dunn RJ et al. Frequent attenders to the ED. The Emergency Medicine Manual.</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Key management principles:<br>Senior ED medical staff (registrar or higher) to see patient<br>To reduce duplication of investigations and management<br>Maintain diagnostic vigilance and avoid cognitive bias related to “frequent presenter” label<br>Do not withhold analgesia while patient being assessed<br>Provide multimodal, opioid-sparing analgesia (e.g. NSAIDs, paracetamol, neuropathic agents, regional techniques where appropriate)<br>Screen for mental health and substance use disorders<br>Utilise individualised management plan with multidisciplinary involvement (eg GP, OT/PT, pain service, gynaecology, mental health)<br>Pregnancy test in all women of reproductive age<br>Limit unnecessary repeat imaging<br>Avoid prescribing/dispensing high-risk medications on discharge from emergency<br> <br>References:<br>Faculty of Pain Medicine ANZCA. Acute Pain Management Guidelines.<br>Tintinalli JE et al., 9th ed.<br>Dunn RJ et al. Frequent attenders to the ED. The Emergency Medicine Manual.</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"Arrhythmia","stemText":"A 68-year-old woman presents with palpitations and dyspnoea, onset approximately 10 hours ago. She has been well recently. She is an asthmatic (uses salbutamol PRN), had an anterior STEMI 3 years ago with stent insertion with her last echocardiogram (12 months ago) showing a LVEF 40%. She takes aspirin and atorvastatin.\n\nHer vials are as follows:\n\nTemp 36.8C\nBP 105/60\nRR 16\nSats 98% on RA.\n\nAn ECG is performed and shown below.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the diagnosis shown on this ECG (1 mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Complete the table below with FOUR (4) different pharmacological agents you would consider in the management of this patient. For each agent state the dose and route, and provide a rationale for either using or avoiding the agent in this specific patient (8 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three (3) considerations for elective cardioversion of this patient in the Emergency Department. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:inla11d059a65ce70047"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Rapid atrial fibrillation. No significant ischaemic changes.</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Drug (dose and route)</strong></th><th><strong>Drug (dose and route)</strong></th><th><strong>Rationale for use or avoidance in this patient</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Metoprolol orally 25mg or 5mg IV</td><td>Avoid IV metoprolol (asthma and borderline BP)<br>Careful low dose beta-blocker could be considered</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Flecainide 1-2 mg/kg or 100 mg IV or oral 200 mg</td><td>Avoid flecanide as structural heart disease and prior MI</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Amiodarone 300 mg IV over an hour</td><td>Safer in LV dysfunction, can cause transient hypotension</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Digoxin 500 mcg oral/IV bolus repeated after 6 hours</td><td>Well tolerated in elderly patient with poor LV as negatively chronotrophic but positively ionotropic<br> </td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Accept 1 option of anticoagulation</td><td>CHADs2Vasc high or post cardioversion for 2- 4 weeks.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Magnesium e.g. 10 mmol in 100 mL over an hour</td><td>Adjunctive rate control agent. Replace deficit if low magnesium. Can cause transent hypotension</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg IV bolus</td><td>Negatively inotropic</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>*Need to have treatments from different classes</em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>1 mark for</strong><br>Onset &lt;48 hours<br><strong>1 mark for any of the following (max 2)</strong><br>No acute precipitant evident on assessment (e.g. sepsis)<br>Signs of heart failure<br>Staff/resource – staff trained and appropriate location. Patient consents<br>Do not accept anticoagulation as stem states patient is on no regular medications<br>Patient consent</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"Unstable Lower GI bleed","stemText":"A 83 year old woman presents to your regional ED with a pre-syncopal episode. She reports 3 days of red blood in her stool. Her medical history includes paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension, ischaemic heart disease with previous NSTEMIs and stent, and cognitive decline. She lives with her husband. Her medications include rivaroxaban, metoprolol, perindopril and amitriptyline.\n\nHer vital signs are:\nRR 20,\nHR 66 (irreg)\nBP 95/50\nT 35.3\nGCS 15\n\nA multiphase CT was requested as part of your assessment. An axial slice is shown below.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the significant finding in this image and provide your interpretation (1 mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State FOUR (4) ways, in which this patient’s background history may influence your management strategy."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Complete the following table with THREE (3) possible management options to gain"}],"propPages":["asset:inl819dc078eb4478cc0"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Arterial blush in ascending colon,  indicating active bleeding  <br>(1/2 mark for location ascending colon, ½ mark for active bleed). </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>1 mark for <br>Frailty/advanced age influencing escalation decisions<br>1 mark for each of (max 3)<br>Cognitive decline – implications for consent/ need for substitute decision maker.  <br>Co-morbidities – depending on background patients cardiac function, she may not be suitable for anaesthetic for open management. This might influence decisions for definitive management.  <br>Anticoagulated – needs reversal of anticoagulation in consultation with haematology.  <br>Beta-blocked – cannot rely on heart rate as a surrogate marker for shock and response to resuscitation.  </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Management option</strong></th><th><strong>Pro</strong></th><th><strong>Con</strong></th></tr><tr><td>Reversal of Anticoagulation</td><td>Rapidly reduces ongoing bleeding<br>Can be initiated immediately in regional ED<br>Non-invasive</td><td>Does not provide definitive haemostasis<br>Thrombotic risk (IHD, AF, coronary stent)\nAndexanet often unavailable/expensive</td></tr><tr><td>Interventional Radiology – Mesenteric Angiography with Embolisation</td><td>Minimally invasive definitive haemostasis<br>Avoids laparotomy<br>High success for arterial bleeding</td><td>Requires transfer to tertiary centre\nDelay in unstable patient<br>Risk of bowel ischaemia<br>Requires contrast (renal risk)</td></tr><tr><td>Surgical Management – Laparotomy ± Bowel Resection</td><td>Definitive control<br>Appropriate if IR unavailable or patient unstable</td><td>High perioperative risk (age, IHD, anticoagulated)<br>Anaesthetic risk<br>Significant morbidity and mortality<br>Risk of a stoma</td></tr><tr><td>Colonoscopy with Endoscopic Therapy</td><td>Diagnostic and therapeutic<br>Less invasive than surgery</td><td>Limited utility in haemodynamic instability<br>Requires bowel prep<br>Difficult with active large-volume bleeding</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"Lower Limb Neuro","stemText":"A 35 year old woman presents to the ED with a letter from her GP asking you to exclude transverse myelitis. The patient reports a 2 day history of some lower limb weakness and difficulty mobilising. She has a history of illicit drug use but no other known medical problems. She is afebrile and systemically well.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Aside from transverse myelitis, List FOUR urgent alternative diagnoses that must be excluded in the ED (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State THREE (3) inclusion criteria for the diagnosis of transverse myelitis? (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Aside from infective or post-infective causes, list TWO (2) other potential causes of transverse myelitis? (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"As part of your assessment you perform a complete neurological examination, the findings are below:\n\nTone: Increased tone in both legs\n\nPower: Bilateral lower limb weakness 3/5 throughout\n\nReflexes: Brisk knee reflexes, upgoing plantar responses bilaterally\n\nSensation: Clearly defined sensory level across the upper abdomen\n\nNormal upper limbs\n\nPost void bladder scan ~400mls\n\nState the level of the localised lesion and provide TWO (2) justifications (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Compressive spinal cord lesions (disc, malignancy, epidural abscess/haematoma)<br>Guillain-Barré syndrome<br>Anterior spinal artery infarction<br>Nitrous oxide abuse</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Diagnostic criteria (Transverse Myelitis Consortium)<br><strong>Inclusion:</strong><br>Development of sensory, motor or autonomic dysfunction attributable to the spinal cord<br>Bilateral signs/symptoms <br>Clearly defined sensory level<br>MRI excludes compressive lesion (CT is not adequate)<br>Spinal cord inflammation demonstrated by CSF pleocytosis, elevated IgG index or gadolinium enhancement on MRI<br>Progression to nadir: 4 hrs – 21 days after symptom onset</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Causes include:<br>1. Idiopathic<br>Up to 30% have no identifiable cause<br>post-vaccination <br>2. CNS autoimmune diseases<br>Multiple sclerosis<br>Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (anti-AQP4)<br>Acute demyelinating encephalomyelitis (ADEM)<br>Myelin Oligodendrocyte Glycoprotein Antibody-associated Disease (MOGAD) <br>Acute flaccid myelitis<br>3. Systemic inflammatory autoimmune disorders<br>Sarcoidosis<br>Sjögren syndrome<br>SLE<br>Less commonly: RA, systemic sclerosis, Behçet’s, etc.<br>4. Paraneoplastic syndromes<br>Most commonly associated with:<br>Small cell lung cancer<br>Antibodies: anti-Hu, CRMP5, anti-amphiphysin</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal upper limbs</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Post void bladder scan ~400mls</div><div class=\"answer-line\">State the level of the localised lesion and provide TWO (2) justifications (3 marks)</div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Thoracic spinal cord lesion at approximately T8 (differentials transverse myelitis, spinal cord compression or cord infarction)<br>Justification<br>UMN signs in both legs → lesion above L1 spinal cord level<br>Bilateral findings → central cord pathology<br>Defined sensory level → spinal cord, not peripheral nerve<br>Normal upper limbs → lesion below cervical enlargement<br>Autonomic dysfunction (bladder) → cord involvement</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"Trauma / ROTEM","stemText":"A 27 year-old male is brought to the Emergency Department after a high-speed motor bike crash. He is haemodynamically unstable and a massive haemorrhage protocol has been activated. You suspect he has intraabdominal bleeding as the cause of his shock.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Outline two (2) scores and list four (4) components within each score that can be used to predict the need for Tranexamic Acid and/or Fibrinogen Concentrate prior to laboratory test result availability. (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Following initial clinical assessment, the patient has a ROTEM performed to help guide coagulation management shown below:\n\nList ONE (1) finding consistent with Trauma Induced Coagulopathy (TIC) demonstrated by the ROTEM image. (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List four (4) benefits of viscoelastic assays when compared to traditional coagulation tests. (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Aside from coagulopathy, complete the following table with the THREE (3) other elements comprising the ‘Diamond of Death’ and provide a management of each (6 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"State the dose of Apheresis Cryoprecipitate for the above patient (weight 80kg). (1 mark)"}],"propPages":["asset:inl003eaaf4b53c45e9c"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Score</th><th>Factors</th></tr><tr><td><strong>TXA:</strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>COAST (Coagulopathy of Severe Trauma)</td><td>Entrapment, hypotension &lt;90 or &lt;100, suspected intraabdominal/pelvic bleeding, major chest injury, hypothermia &lt;32 or &lt;35 deg</td></tr><tr><td>BATT (Bleeding Audit Triage Trauma)</td><td>Age &gt;65 &gt;75, SBP &lt;60 &lt;100, GCS &lt;8, RR&gt;30, HR &gt;100, penetrating or high velocity trauma mechanism</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fibrinogen:</strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>FibAT (Fibrinogen on Admission in Trauma)</td><td>Age &lt;33, hypotension &lt;100mmHg, hypothermia &lt;36deg, tachycardia HR &gt; 100, FF on FAST, reduction in Hb &gt;20, initial Hb &lt;120, lactate &gt;2.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Hypofibrinogenemia</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Faster result &lt; 10 minutes vs &gt;45 minutes<br>Define specific factor replacement required (individualised patient care)<br>Reduce product utilisation<br>Can be performed at the bedside<br>May predict ongoing bleeding risk</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Element</strong></th><th><strong>Specific Management</strong></th></tr><tr><td>Temperature control</td><td>Keep patient warm- Active internal warming (warm fluids), external passive warming devices (Bair Hugger) and warm room</td></tr><tr><td>Hypocalcaemia</td><td>Proactive administration of 10mmol CaGluc with each unit of product until VBG available</td></tr><tr><td>Acidaemia</td><td>Correct hypoperfusion with volume replacement and manage shock state</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>9 -10 units</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 18</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"Resp - Asthma","stemText":"You are the duty consultant in a tertiary emergency department. A 23-year-old female patient presents at triage with respiratory compromise. She has a known history of poorly controlled asthma, is a non-smoker, and has no other significant past medical history.\n\nThe triage nurse has commenced oxygen delivery via low-flow nasal cannula at 6L/min, but the patient has received no other treatment on their arrival to the resus cubicle.\n\nHer arterial blood gas is shown below\nFIO2 0.45\npH 7.295\npCO2 63 mmHg\npO2 36 mmHg\nHCO3 29.8 mmol/L\nNa 131 mmol/L\nK 3.8 mmol/L\nCa 1.22 mmol/L\nCl 96 mmol/L\nLactate 1.2 mmol/L","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the TWO (2) major abnormalities on this arterial blood gas. (1 mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State your clinical interpretation of these findings. (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State you FIVE (5) immediate management steps. (5 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Despite your initial management, the patient continues to deteriorate, and you decide to intubate.\n\nOutline 5 (FIVE) modifications you would make to your intubation/ventilation strategy in this patient. (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>8</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Severe hypoxia (P/F Ratio 36/0.45 = 80)<br>Respiratory Acidosis (pH &lt;7.35, hypercapnia)<br><br>Needs both findings (not calculations) for mark</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Critical/Life-threatening asthma with evidence of tiring</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Increased oxygen delivery via e.g. 15LNRBM/ HFNPO2 <br>Nebulized Salbutamol 5mg<br>Nebulized Ipratropium 500microg<br>Hydrocortisone 200mg IV<br><br>One of: <br>MgSO4 10-20mmol IV<br>Antibiotic cover – Ceftriaxone 1gram IV, Azithromycin 500mg IV<br>Accept aminophylline/adrenaline/ketamine with reasonable dose<br><br><em>do not accept MDI therapy in this critically unwell patient</em></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Ketamine for induction<br>Delayed-sequence intubation with NIV<br>Largest possible ETT<br>Low/Zero PEEP<br>Low respiratory rate<br>Increased I:E ratio (1:3/1:4)<br>Low tidal volume (5ml/kg)<br>Volume controlled ventilation mode<br>Tolerance of high airway pressures<br>Tolerance of hypercapnia/acidaemia</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"Paeds head injury","stemText":"A 4-year-old girl presents to the ED after falling from a kitchen bench onto a tiled floor, striking her occiput. She cried immediately. Since the injury, she has vomited three times and has developed an occipital scalp haematoma. The intern asks you about head injury clinical decision rules to determine whether CT imaging is required.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"In a 4-year-old child, list FOUR (4) high-risk features that would mandate CT brain imaging according to validated paediatric clinical decision rules (4 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You are reassured after your initial assessment and admit the child to the Short Term Treatment Area for observation. Two hours later, you are informed her parents are concerned that her behaviour is not normal. On review, you decide that a CT brain is required. The child is unlikely to remain still and requires sedation to facilitate the scan.\n\nComplete the below table with TWO (2) appropriate sedation strategies for this child. For each provider: dose and route; one advantage in this context; and one important risk or limitation (6 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The CT brain is reported as normal. After a further period of observation in STTA, the patient is ready for discharge.\n\nList FOUR essential criteria that must be met prior to discharge (2 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>PECARN, PREDCIT &amp; CATCH<br>GCS &lt;15 at 2 hours post injury<br>Signs of basilar or palpable skull fracture<br>Worsening signs or symptoms<br>Altered mental state<br>Large boggy scalp haematoma<br><br>CHALICE (consider CT) - only accept 1 as there don’t mandate a CT<br>Witnessed LOC &gt;5 mins<br>Hx of amnesia &gt;5mins<br>Persistent vomiting ≥3 episodes<br>Suspicion of NAI<br>Seizure with no hx of epilepsy<br>Severe mechanism (fall &gt;1.5 m, high-speed MVC)<br></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Drug (route, dose)</strong></th><th><strong>Drug (route, dose)</strong></th><th></th><th></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Ketamine<br>IV 0.5 - 1 mg/kg<br>IM 4mg/kg</td><td>Advantage</td><td> Haemodynamic stability; preserves airway reflexes; short acting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Ketamine<br>IV 0.5 - 1 mg/kg<br>IM 4mg/kg</td><td>Risk / limitation</td><td> Laryngospasm; vomiting; emergence; requires IV access and skilled sedationist; monitoring / more resource intensive</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Dexmedetomidine<br>IN 2–4 mcg/kg</td><td>Advantage</td><td>Minimal respiratory depression; non-invasive</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Dexmedetomidine<br>IN 2–4 mcg/kg</td><td>Risk / limitation</td><td>Slower onset; bradycardia; may be insufficient for motionless CT</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Midazolam<br>IN/buccal 0.3mg/kg </td><td>Advantage</td><td>Relatively quick onset; readily available; familiarity; anxiolytic; non-invasive </td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Midazolam<br>IN/buccal 0.3mg/kg </td><td>Risk / limitation</td><td>Risk paradoxical excitation; hypotension; apnoea; potenitally insufficient as single agent </td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Clonidine<br>PO 1-4mcg/kg</td><td>Advantage</td><td>Sedation, analgesia &amp; anxiolytic; </td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Clonidine<br>PO 1-4mcg/kg</td><td>Risk / limitation</td><td>Slower onset; bradycardia / hypotension (generally well tolerated); less familiarity ; medication required to be crushed -&gt; risk of dose error</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>GCS 15 and behaviour normal for ≥1 hour<br>No ongoing vomiting or neurological deterioration<br>Normal CT (if performed)<br>Responsible adult with clear return advice<br>No other factors warranting admission or further observation e.g., no NAI concerns, no other injuries or underlying medication conditions requiring management<br>Tolerating oral intake</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"CVA Lysis with bleed","stemText":"A 79-year-old man presents via ambulance to your regional emergency department with right sided weakness and speech disturbance. Paramedics report he was last seen well by his wife 2 hours prior. He has had no pre-hospital interventions or treatment initiated.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State THREE (3) critical parts of your initial clinical assessment (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You perform a brief initial neurological examination with findings as below:\n\n• GCS 13 (E4 V3 M6)\n\n• Right sided weakness with 3/5 power in upper limb, 4+/5 lower limb\n\n• Right sided facial droop\n\n• Profound global aphasia\n\n• Visual fields and sensation unable to be tested due to aphasia\n\nState the most likely vessel involved? (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"His wife has now arrived and informs you that the patient is a retired engineer with an active social life, still drives and plays golf weekly.\n\nYou note there are no contraindications to thrombolysis and that aeromedical retrieval to a Stroke or Interventional Radiology centre is over 2 hours away.\n\nComplete the following table with 2 pros and 2 cons of thrombolysis therapy? (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"45 minutes after commencement of thrombolysis, the patient begins to vomit and his GCS drops to 9 (E2 V2 M5). You suspect he has a significant intracranial haemorrhage secondary to thrombolysis\n\nState your FIVE (5) immediate steps to manage this scenario (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><div class=\"answer-pass-mark\">Pass mark: <strong>10</strong></div><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Secure IV access for contrast scan<br>Check BSL <br>Baseline neurological examination <br>Calculation of NIHSS score<br>Arrange urgent CT head (stroke procotol – non con CT/CTA/perfusion) <br>Activate “Code Stroke” (or similar local protocol) </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Left middle cerebral artery </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Pro of thrombolysis</strong></th><th><strong>Pro of thrombolysis</strong></th><th><strong>Con of thrombolysis</strong></th><th><strong>Con of thrombolysis</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Return to functional independence post thrombolysis has NNT = 10 </td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Risk of significant ICH with thrombolysis NNTH = 42</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Immediately available therapy in ED </td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Risk of death secondary to sICH NNTH 122</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Given the severe symptom burden, any benefit secondary to thrombolysis may be significant</td><td></td><td>There is disagreement in the literature about the evidence </td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>No mortality benefit </td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Expect there will be answers to do with the transfer, which should be accepted given the prompt in the stem </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Cease r-tPA (thrombolysis) infusion<br>Investigations: urgent non con CT head; blood tests: FBC, coagulation profile, crossmatch <br>Reverse thrombolysis – 10units cryoprecipitate, 2units FFP, 1g TXA (to reverse fibrinolysis); 1unit platelets (to reverse anti-platelet) <br>Neuroprotective measures – head up 30 degrees, target PaCO2 35-40 mmHg, antiemetics, consideration of osmotic therapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline); sedation +/- RSI <br>Neurosurgical and Haemology consult  <br>Secure threatened airway with RSI if consistent with goals of care <br>Disposition dependent on establishing ceiling of care – consider palliation vs ICU and transfer to neurosurgical centre </th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Resources:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ACEM, Statement on Intravenous Thrombolysis for Ischaemic Stroke, November 2025, accessed 11/01/2026 via </div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"Geriatric blunt chest trauma","stemText":"A 80year old female patient from a Residential Aged Care facility presents following an unwitnessed fall from standing height in the bathroom. They sustained blunt left sided chest trauma from impact with the sink.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the table below with two (2) specific considerations you would make for a geriatric trauma patient for each component of a primary survey (8 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"A trauma pan-scan (CT Head/ C-Spine/ Chest+ Abdomen + Pelvis + C) was performed which revealed isolated 3 undisplaced left sided rib fractures (7/8/9 ribs).\n\nState FOUR (4) features on assessment that would guide your disposition for this patient (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Primary Survey</strong></th><th><strong>Consideration for a Geriatric Patient</strong></th><th><strong>Consideration for a Geriatric Patient</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>Airway</strong></td><td>1</td><td>Risk obstruction due to friable tissue and false teeth<br>Cervical arthritis- danger extending the neck</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Airway</strong></td><td>2</td><td>TMJ arthritis- reduced mouth opening<br>Flexion deformities neck difficult intubation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Breathing</strong></td><td>1</td><td>Weakened respiratory muscles/ osteoporotic ribs- increased rib &amp; sternal fractures fractures<br>Reduction VC/FRV/FEV limit respiratory compensation- therefore rib fractures/ pulmonary contusions more problematic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Breathing</strong></td><td>2</td><td>Blunted response to hypoxia and hypercarbia<br>Pre-existing lung pathology (i.e. COPD) reduced physiological reserve</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Circulation</strong></td><td>1</td><td>Normal tachycardic response to pain/ hypotension blunted, especially if on B-blockers<br>Increased bleeding risk due to antiplatelets/ anticoagulation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Circulation</strong></td><td>2</td><td>Pre-existing hypertension misleading BP, therefore hypovolaemia may occur in setting of normal blood pressure<br>Type 2 MI may result from underperfusoin/ oxygenation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability</strong></td><td>1</td><td>Agitation/ low GCS may be difficult to assess with alterations to baseline cognitive function<br>Cortical atrophy may delay clinical manifestations of serous intracranial hemorrhage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability</strong></td><td>2</td><td>Hypothermia is more common, increasing bleeding risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1 x mark may be allocated to occult pathology causation of fall i.e. myocardial ischemia/ arrythmia, seizure, ICH, CVA, hyponatremia, hypoglycaemia. Other sensible answers may be considered</em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Patient is unable to cough/ take deep break/ mobilise despite adequate analgesia<br>Pre-existing lung disease- smoker/ COPD/ asthma/ immunosuppression as increased risk of pneumonia (increased by 27%, and mortality increased by 19% for each rib fracture)<br>Other co-morbidities- high frailty score/ morbid obesity/ congestive cardiac failure/ poor premorbid function<br>Respiratory compromise- Tachypnoea, hypoxia, increased WOB<br>Occult pathology identified as causation of fall<br>Collaboration with families and allied health for identification of barriers to discharge</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":19,"title":"SBP","stemText":"A 55 year old male cirrhotic patient with known ascites has presented to your Emergency Department with abdominal pain. You suspect Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List TWO (2) pathogens that commonly cause Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (2 Marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Apart from abdominal pain, list TWO (2) indications to perform an ascitic tap to investigate for Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (2 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"You perform an ascitic tap\n\nComplete the following table with THREE (3) investigations you would send the ascitic fluid for. For each provide a value that would diagnose or indicate Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (6 Marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State TWO (2) features on either clinical assessment or on ascitic fluid analysis that would help to distinguish between primary and secondary bacterial peritonitis (2 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>enteric bacteria <br>Escherichia coli<br>Klebsiella pneumonia<br>Enterococcus faecalis<br>Enterococcus faecium <br>also accept staph</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>eTG<br>Fever<br>Hypothermia<br>Encephalopathy<br>Septic shock<br>Deteriorating renal function<br>UTD (additional to above)<br>Altered mental status<br>?lab findings may indicate peripheral leukocytosis, metabolic acidosis &amp; new hypoNa<br>Acute GI bleed </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Number</strong></th><th><strong>Test</strong></th><th><strong>Positive result</strong></th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Cell Count*</strong></td><td><strong>absolute neutrophil count ≥250 cells/</strong><strong>microL</strong><strong> </strong><strong>or  0.25</strong><strong> x 10^9/L*</strong></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gram Stain</td><td>Positive for organism</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Culture</td><td>Isolated organism</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Albumin</td><td>serum-ascites albumin gradient ≥1.1 g/dL</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Total Protein</td><td>Protein concentration &lt;1 g/dL</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Glucose</td><td>Glucose concentration generally remains above 50 mg/dL (2.8 mmol/L).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>LDH</td><td>Elevated</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Amylase</td><td>Amylase in ascitic fluid in SBP is usually less than serum amylase.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Bilirubin</td><td>Bilirubin conc is typically low in SBP and less than serum bilirubin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>*2 Marks </em><em>for  Cell</em><em> Count and Correct neutrophil count </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>4 Marks for any 2 of the others </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Secondary Peritonitis<br>More unstable<br>Greater abdominal pain<br>Ascitic fluid in secondary<br>Higher protein &gt;1g/dL<br>Lower glucose &lt;2.8<br>Bile stained &gt;6<br>Very high neutrophil count &gt;4000<br>Polymicrobial on culture or on Gm Stain<br>Elevated amylase</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":20,"title":"PPH","stemText":"A 32-year-old woman, G1P1 at 40 weeks gestation has been brought in by ambulance having delivered a baby at home 45 minutes ago. She has received no antenatal care and was in labour for 12 hours prior to delivering a healthy baby girl, after which the doula called 000 due to significant PV bleeding.\n\nThe paramedics estimate blood loss since the delivery was around 1000mL, for which they have inserted a 22G PIVC and given 500mL N/saline enroute. The newborn is being cared for by a paediatrician in the next cubicle.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State FOUR (4) specific risk factors for primary PPH (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Her vital signs are\n\nHR 120bpm\n\nBP 85/55\n\nRR 24/min\n\nSpO2 96% RA\n\nTemp 36.9C\n\nGCS 14 (M6 V5 E3).\n\nShe has been placed on a resus bed and cardiorespiratory monitoring applied. Upon transfer you note she appears pale and that she is continuing to bleed onto the bluey on the bed.\n\nState your TEN (10) key management steps for this patient (10 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Age &gt; 35yo<br>Blood dyscrasias<br>Placenta praevia<br>Prolonged labour<br>Maternal Sepsis<br>Severe pre-eclampsia<br>Polyhydramnios/ macrosomia/ multiple pregnancy  (leads to overdistended uterus)<br>Previous Hx PPH/ RPOC<br>Traumatic delivery – uterine rupture, cervical tear, vaginal tear, perineal tear (accept 1)<br>Drug-induced hypotonic uterus (MgSO4)</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">She has been placed on a resus bed and cardiorespiratory monitoring applied. Upon transfer you note she appears pale and that she is continuing to bleed onto the bluey on the bed.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">State your TEN (10) key management steps for this patient (10 marks)</div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>General Resuscitation (1 mark)</strong><br>Call for help / activate obstetric emergency response (incl. senior obstetrics, anaesthetics, midwifery, blood bank/haematology).<br>IV access: 2 × large-bore IV / rapid infuse<br>Others: Airway and breathing optimization, with administration of 15L NRBM O2, Lie supine in bed <br><br><strong>Specific Resuscitation (2 marks)</strong><br>Activate Massive Transfusion Protocol (MTP) <br>Blood products in balanced ratios (RBC + FFP + platelets) guided by MTP/ROTEM, with permissive hypotensive targets<br>Others: Haemostatic resuscitation with warmed IV fluids aiming for permissive hypotension SBP 100mmHg until group specific or O neg blood available for transfusion<br><br><strong>Specific management (6 Marks)</strong><br><br>Tone (2 marks): <br>Bimanual uterine massage<br>Uterotonics: oxytocin 5 IU IV repeated at 5minutes followed by IV infusion 5-10 IU/h, ergometrine 250mcg IV + 1000mg misprostol PR<br><br>Tissue (1 Mark):<br>inspect placenta; manual removal/evacuation<br><br>Trauma (1 Mark):<br>examine cervix/vagina/perineum &amp; repair lacerations;<br><br>Thrombin / coagulopathy (2 Marks)<br>1g IV TXA over 10 minutes<br>‘Correct coagulopathy’ but must mention a specific factor for the scenario i.e Fibrinogen replacement (cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate) or Calcium replacement during MTP<br><br><strong>Disposition (1 Mark)</strong><br>transfer to operating theatre and/or ICU/HDU with ongoing haemorrhage control and haemostatic monitoring</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":21,"title":"Epiglottitis","stemText":"A 3 year old child is brought to ED with their parents with a sore throat. On examination, they are sitting up, quiet and drooling.\n\nVital signs are:\nTemp 38.7\nHR 165\nRR 40\nSpO2 99% in room air","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the following table with FOUR (4) differential diagnoses for this child’s presentation. For each provide a key historical and a key examination"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"A lateral soft tissue neck x-ray was performed and shown below\n\nState the diagnosis (1 Mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Besides airway obstruction, list THREE life-threatening complications of this pathology (3 Marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:inl1e935894175df5be8"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Diagnosis</strong></th><th><strong>Diagnosis</strong></th><th><strong>Feature</strong></th><th><strong>Feature</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Epiglottitis</td><td>On History</td><td>Sudden onset high fever <br>Rapid progression over hours <br>Absence of preceding coryzal illness<br>Unvaccinated</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Epiglottitis</td><td>On Exam</td><td>Muffled voice<br>Looks toxic<br>Tripoding or sniffing position<br>Minimal cough</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Retropharyngeal abscess</td><td>On History</td><td>Insidious onset over days after URTI<br>Dysphagia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Retropharyngeal abscess</td><td>On Exam</td><td>Neck swelling <br>Torticollis / hyperextension <br>Bulging posterior pharynx</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Inhaled FB</td><td>On History</td><td>Choking episode at onset of symptoms<br>No prodrome</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Inhaled FB</td><td>On Exam</td><td>Unilateral reduced air entry or focal wheeze</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Croup</td><td>On History</td><td>Corysal prodrome<br>Nocturnal worsening</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Croup</td><td>On Exam</td><td>Inspiratory stridor <br>Classic seal barking cough<br>NOn-toxic</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Angioedema</td><td>On History</td><td>History of atopy / anaphylaxis<br>Acute onset after allergen exposure</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Angioedema</td><td>On Exam</td><td>Oropharyngeal swelling<br>Shock<br>Urticaria<br>Wheeze</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Key feature implies a feature that will differentiate between diagnoses, therefore answers like ‘hoarse voice’ should not be accepted as it would be present in multiple potential diagnoses</em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Retropharyngeal abscess</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Septic shock<br>Internal jugular vein thrombosis (Lemierre syndrome)<br>Carotid artery erosion<br>Descending necrotising mediastinitis<br>Epidural abscess<br>Aspiration pneumonia</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":22,"title":"Native Valve Endocarditis","stemText":"A 24 year old male presents with fevers, weight loss and confusion. He is a MSM and also injects amphetamines intravenously. After assessing the patient you suspect infective endocarditis. The patient has no prior cardiac history. They have been given 2L of crystalloid by the night registrar.\n\nHis vitals are:\nHR 105\nBP 93/50\nTemp 39\nSats 94% OA\nRR 22","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the name of the most commonly used criteria to diagnose endocarditis (1 Mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the TWO (2) minor criteria that this patient meets (2 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List THREE (3) additional clinical examination findings that if one was present would allow a diagnosis of ‘possible endocarditis’ to be made (3 Marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State your antibiotic regime for this patient? (3 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List the THREE (3) likely organisms be isolated from this patient;s blood cultures (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Duke Criteria</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>IVDU<br>Fever &gt;38.0C</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Vascular phenomena: <br>Conjunctival haemorrhage<br>Janeway lesion<br>Purulent purpura<br>Splinter haemorrhage <br>Immunological phenomena: <br>Osler nodes <br>Roth spots<br><br>2023 update states further minor criteria for predisposition to IE, therefore other acceptable answers would be:<br>Evidence of previous cardiac surgery <br>Auscultation of mechanical heart valve sounds<br>Clinical signs of HOCM: harsh apical systolic murmur, jerky rapid pulse &amp; prominent left ventricular impulse<br>Further new minor criteria:<br>New valvular regurgitation on auscultation (where echo not available)<br><br>2023-The-2023-Duke-ISCVID-Criteria-for-Infective-Endocarditis-Fowler-CID-May-4-2023-Final.pdf</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Vancomycin 1.5-2g (25mg/kg)<br>Flucloxacillin 2g<br>Tobramycin or Gentamicin 5 -7mg/kg<br><br>Benzyl Penicillin is incorrect in this case.  ETG state should be replaced with vancomycin if sepsis or septic shock or patients with high MRSA risk.  ie This patient.</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Staph aureus (need to mention staph aureus to pass, do no need to specify MRSA or MSSA)<br>Strep viridans, streptococci ok as a stand alone answer.<br>Enterococci<br>HACEK group (<em>Haemophilus, Actinobacillus, </em><em>Cardiobacterium</em><em>, </em><em>Eikenella</em><em>, </em><em>Kingella</em>)<br>Candida/Psuedomonas also ok in IVDU</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong> / 12 </strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":23,"title":"Hyperthermic toxidrome","stemText":"A 35 year old male is brought into your emergency department by ambulance.\n\nHis vital signs are\nHR 140\nBP 180/100\nT 39.\n\nYou perform an initial assessment and believe the most likely diagnosis is a toxidrome.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the table with the expected changed in the three (3) toxidromes (12 Marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th></th><th><strong>Serotonin Syndrome</strong></th><th><strong>Neuroleptic Malignant </strong></th><th><strong>Anticholinergic Syndrome</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>Skin</strong></td><td>Diaphoretic/sweaty </td><td>Diaphoretic/ sweaty, pale</td><td>Dry, flushed, hot </td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pupils</strong></td><td>Mydriasis/dilated</td><td>Normal <br>Can be dilated/ mydriasis</td><td>Mydriasis/dilated</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reflexes</strong></td><td>Hyperreflexia, clonus </td><td>Bradyreflexia  </td><td>Normal reflexes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mental Status</strong></td><td>Agitated, anxiety, delirium progressing to coma </td><td>Mutism, staring, stupor, bradykinesia, coma</td><td>Agitated, delirium, confusion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":24,"title":"Returned Traveller","stemText":"A 36 year old man returns from a three week holiday to South East Asia. He complains of diarrhoea which started overseas and is still going five days later. He is usually well.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List FOUR (4) pathogens are involved in Traveller’s Diarrhoea? (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State FOUR (4) clinical features suggest invasive bacterial traveller’s diarrhoea? (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient has features of invasive bacterial diarrhoea.\n\nState TWO (2) possible antibiotic regimens for severe traveller’s diarrhoea. (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>1 mark for</strong><br><strong>E Coli</strong> – enteroinvasive or enterotoxigenic<br>1 mark for each of the following (max 3)<br>Shigella<br>Salmonella<br>Rotavirus<br>Norovirus<br>Campylobacter<br>Vibrio<br>Giardia</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>1 mark for each of the following (max 2 marks) as these are the key features</strong><br>High fever (&gt;38C)<br>Bloody diarrhoea<br><strong>1 mark for each of the following (max 2 marks)</strong><br>Systemically unwell (examiner judgement if they use a range of terms to show this),<br>Mucus in stool<br>Severe abdominal pain<br>Tachycardia<br>High volume diarrhoea with signs of hypovolaemia<br>Shock</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Ciprofloxacin PO 750 mg, then 500mg bd for two more days<br>Azithromycin PO 1g, then 500mg od for two more days<br>Norfloxacin PO 800mg, then 400mg bd for two more days<br>(Note, for severe disease needs the two extra days, single dose is not sufficient. Mark for drug and initial dose, plus the ongoing two days.)<br>(Note, Dunn suggests Bactrim could be used, but doesn’t give doses. However Bactrim does not feature in eTG – maybe could allow half a mark.)</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":25,"title":"DKA","stemText":"An 8 year old male presents to the ED with abdominal pain, vomiting, and weight loss for the past 3 weeks. His initial finger prick BGL reads “hi”. You suspect a first presentation of DKA.\n\nHe weighs 25 kg.\n\nVitals:\nHR 110\nRR 40\nSpO2 99% RA\nTemp 37.0\nBP 98/50","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the THREE (3) specific biochemical values to diagnose DKA (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Below is his VBG\n\nTest Result Ref Range\n\npH 7.01 7.32 - 7.43\n\npCO2 18 38 - 54 mmHg\n\nHCO3 2 30 - 32 mmol/L\n\nCl 120 95 - 110 mmol/L\n\nNa 146 135 -145 mmol/L\n\nK 4.0 3.5 - 5.2 mmol/L\n\nGlu 35 3.0 - 7.8 mmol/L\n\nLac 3.7 0.5 -2.2 mmol/L\n\nKetones 5.2\n\nInterpret the VBG and state THREE (3) key abnormalities. (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State THREE (3) key principles of IV fluid replacement in children who present with moderate to severe DKA. (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State at what point you would commence the insulin infusion and at what rate (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>BGL &gt; 11 mmol/L<br>pH &lt; 7.3 and/or bicarb &lt; 18<br>Ketones&gt; 3 mmol/L or ketonuria Ketones &gt; 2+ on urinalysis<br><em>(QCH and RCH)</em><br><br><em>Standard mark - 3</em></th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Below is his VBG</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">\tGlu\t\t\t35\t\t\t3.0 - 7.8 mmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">\tLac\t\t\t3.7\t\t\t0.5  -2.2 mmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">\tKetones \t\t5.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Interpret the VBG and state THREE (3) key abnormalities. (4 marks) </div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Interpretation </strong>(1 Mark)<br>Severe raised anion gap (AG = 24) metabolic acidosis likely secondary to DKA and elevated lactate – <em>need a coherent interpretation + any of the below 3 points</em><br><br><strong>Key abnormalities </strong>(3 Marks)<br>Not appropriately compensated - expected CO2 = 11<br>Hypernatraemia - Corrected Na = 156 <br>Nac = Nam + [(Glu – 5)/3] <br>Expected K =  7 🡪 indicates significant potassium deficit in context of acidaemia<br>Hyperlactaemia/elevated lactate – multifactorial in DKA including poor tissue perfusion (hypovolaemia), stress induced catcholamine release, metabolic defragment in glucose handling<br><br><em>Standard mark - 3</em><br></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>ShIf clinically shocked/severely dehydrated give fluid bolus 10/mL/kg Sodium Chloride 0.9%, repeat to maximum of 20mL/kg<br>Calculate and replace deficits over 48 hours + plus maintenance<br>Fluid choice for deficits + maintenance: 1L Sodium Chloride 0.9% + 40mmol KCL provided child’s K+ is &lt; 5.5mmoL/L and child is passing urine<br>Once BGL &lt; 15mmol/L, change to Sodium Chloride 0.9% + 5% glucose and KCL (up to 60mmol in a 1L bag) as required<br><br>1 mark for shock / bolus<br>1 mark for calculating fluid deficits &amp; maintenance with K provided<br>1 mark add in glucose <br><br><em>Standard mark - 2</em></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>1 hour after starting fluids <br>Accept anything in range 0.05 – 0.1U/Kg = 1.25 – 2.5 U/hr<br><br><em>Standard </em><em>mark  -</em><em> 2</em></th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":26,"title":"Dynamic hyperinflation","stemText":"An 18 year old unknown male has been brought in with GHB overdose to your tertiary emergency department requiring intubation for airway protection. An RSI was performed using ketamine, fentanyl, and rocuronium. 10 minutes after intubation, the ventilator’s peak pressure alarm is triggered with peak pressures reaching 50 cmH2O. You notice an urticarial rash, tongue angioedema and auscultate bilateral expiratory wheeze.\n\nYou suspect anaphylaxis.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Apart from anaphylaxis, list FOUR (4) alternative causes of the high airway pressures in this scenario? (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You notice the following pattern on the flow curve of the ventilator\n\nState the underlying pathophysiology and what immediate action will you take? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"In addition to the above, the patient receives adrenaline 500mcg IM x 2 (1,000 mcg total) and 1L 0.9% normal saline with some improvement but ongoing wheeze and poor air entry bilaterally. His vitals are as below:\n\nHR 120\n\nBP 140/100\n\nSpO2 80% on FiO2 30%\n\nETCO2 50\n\nTemperature 37.0C\n\nComplete the table below regarding the ventilator settings that you would adjust for this patient. Provide a rationale for each (5 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State THREE other treatments for this patient (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:inl189b720d6a43bb244"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Pneumothorax<br>Right main bronchus intubation (OR migration of the tube to right main bronchus)<br>Obstruction of the ETT<br>Malfunctioning ventilator<br>Brochospasm<br>Dynamic hyperinflation</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Pathophysiology</strong><br>Dynamic hyperinflation (OR gas-trapping, OR stacked breaths)<br><br><strong>Immediate management</strong><br>Disconnect the ventilator and manually decompress the chest / allow full passive exhalation</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">ETCO2 \t\t50</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Temperature \t37.0C</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Complete the table below regarding the ventilator settings that you would adjust for this patient. Provide a rationale for each (5 marks)</div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>Parameter</strong></th><th><strong>Current Setting</strong></th><th><strong>Adjusted Setting</strong></th><th><strong>Rationale</strong></th></tr><tr><td><strong>FiO2</strong></td><td>0.3</td><td>1.0</td><td>Maximise O2 delivery to correct hypoxaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Resp Rate</strong></td><td>16 breaths/min</td><td>6 - 10 breaths/min</td><td>Reduce RR and allow permissive hypercapnia to increase time for expiration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tidal Volume</strong></td><td>8ml/kg</td><td>6ml/kg</td><td>Minimise tidal volume to allow full expiration and prevent gas trapping</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PEEP</strong></td><td>7 cmH2O </td><td>0-5 cmH2O</td><td>Minimise PEEP to lower risk of barotrauma in high pressure environment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I:E</strong><strong> Ratio</strong></td><td>1:2</td><td>1:3 - 1:4</td><td>Allow more expiration time to prevent gas trapping</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Adrenaline infusion (0.1 – 0.5 mcg/kg/min, or other reasonable dose) targeting improvement in wheeze, air entry, and airway pressures<br>Salbutamol 5mg nebulized Q20min (i.e. back to back) &amp; Ipratropium 500mcg nebulized Q20min (i.e. back to back as a start)<br>Hydrocortisone 100mg IV (OR Dexamethasone 10mg IV)<br>Changing sedation to ketamine (bronchodilator)<br>Deepen sedation<br><br>Has to be specific to the anaphylaxis as only 3 marks. NOT to accept general post intubation cares (e.g. head up 30 degrees, NGT, IDC, maintain normothermia, VTE prophylaxis etc.)</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":27,"title":"RV Strain","stemText":"A 32-year-old woman is brought in by ambulance following an episode of presyncope. She is currently 28-weeks pregnant (G1P0) and had arrived at the international terminal from a long-haul flight. On arrival to ED, she reports having felt shorter of breath than usual during the flight. No chest pain, no ongoing dizziness, no cough.\n\nHer examination findings are as follows:\nBP 134/68 mmHg\nHR 115 bpm\nRR 24 bpm\nSpO₂ 96% on room air\nT 36.8°C\nGCS 15\n\nNo increased work of breathing. HSNDM, chest: clear. Mildly swollen ankles, non-tender calves.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List FOUR (4) differential diagnoses for acute dyspnoea in this patient. (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"A bedside point-of-care US is performed (still images as below):\n\nList THREE (3) features of RV strain as demonstrated on bedside ultrasound. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List THREE (3) other causes that can give this sonographic appearance. (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Your provisional diagnosis is pulmonary embolism with RV strain.\n\nOutline your ongoing management of this patient. (4 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:inlf323921f9175b69cc"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Pulmonary embolism<br>Physiological dyspnoea of pregnancy<br>Respiratory causes: asthma exacerbation, pneumonia or viral infection<br>Anaemia<br>PE is the diagnosis not to miss. Physiological dyspnoea and anaemia are common at this gestation. Asthma and infection are frequent in women of childbearing age. Mid-pregnancy reduces likelihood of peripartum cardiomyopathy and amniotic fluid embolism. Pre-eclampsia is a valid but less common cause of acute dyspnoea at 22 weeks, usually via pulmonary oedema, and should be considered if there is persistent hypertension or other clinical features. Anxiety / panic disorder is a diagnosis of exclusion.  </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>RV dilatation (RV:LV ratio &gt; 1)<br>McConnell’s sign (akinesia or mid RV free wall with preserved apical contraction) or global hypokinesis of RV free wall<br>Septal flattening (D-sign on parasternal short axis; septum bows into the LV)<br>Plethoric IVC / reduced or absent inspiratory collapse<br>Other acceptable answers: <br>Tricuspid regurgitation<br>Reduced TAPSE (Tricuspid annular plane systolic excursion) – marker of impaired RV longitudinal function<br>Acute pulmonary embolism can cause sudden RV pressure overload leading to RV dilatation, septal flattening, impaired RV systolic function, and a plethoric IVC. These findings support the diagnosis of haemodynamically significant PE. Physiological changes of pregnancy, suboptimal windows, chronic pulmonary hypertension, or pre-existing cardiac disease can mimic RV strain. POCUS cannot rule out PE and should be interpreted in clinical context and alongside definitive imaging where appropriate.</th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Pulmonary embolism<br>Right ventricular infarction<br>Acute decompensation of chronic pulmonary hypertension<br>In addition to PE, similar appearances may be seen with chronic pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular myocardial infarction, severe hypoxic lung disease, and tricuspid valve pathology, congenital heart disease eg ASD or PV stenosis  </th></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th>Confirm diagnosis with CTPA or V/Q scan depending on availability and local protocol<br>Commence therapeutic low molecular weight heparin (e.g. enoxaparin)<br>Avoid warfarin and DOACs due to teratogenicity<br>Admit for cardiac monitoring – risk of decompensation with RV strain<br>Consult obstetrics and haematology early</th></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><th><strong>/ 12</strong></th></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false}]);
